inated deep inside the tree.\n\n* **Store’s listener**  \n  When `doc.attach(store)` is called, the Store adds a handler like:\n\n  ```js\n  doc.on(\"delta\", (delta, origin) => this._handleDelta(delta, origin));\n  ```\n\n  The handler receives the delta object (`{set:{title:\"Hello\"}}`) and the *origin* Item (`child`). No path string is needed because the origin itself carries the structural information required to reconstruct the nesting.\n\n---\n\n**3. How Store computes the line to append to the `.jsonl` file**\n\nThe Store does **not** rely on a textual path (e.g. `\"data.section.title\"`). Instead it uses the *nesting chain* that the framework maintains for every Item. The algorithm is roughly:\n\n```js\n_handleDelta(delta, origin) {\n  // 1️⃣ Determine the nesting chain from the Store’s root (doc) down to the origin.\n  const chain = this._nestingFromRootTo(origin);   // → array of Items/Lists/Maps\n\n  // 2️⃣ Build a *selector* that describes where the change happened.\n  //    For each step we record the *key* or *index* that was used to reach the next step.\n  //    The selector is an array of “steps”, each step being either:\n  //      - {type:\"prop\", key:\"data\"}\n  //      - {type:\"map\", key:\"section\"}\n  //      - {type:\"list\", index:3}\n  const selector = chain.map(step => ({\n    type: step._type,               // \"prop\", \"map\", or \"list\"\n    key:  step._key,                // for prop/map\n    index: step._index              // for list\n  }));\n\n  // 3️⃣ Combine the selector with the delta payload.\n  //    The line format used by the framework is a JSON object with two top‑level fields:\n  //      - \"sel\": the selector (array of steps)\n  //      - \"op\":  the delta payload (the mutation description)\n  const lineObj = {\n    sel: selector,\n    op: delta\n  };\n\n  // 4️⃣ Serialize and append.\n  const line = JSON.stringify(lineObj);\n  this._appendLine(line);\n}\n```\n\n**Key points that avoid a path string:**\n\n* **`_nestingFromRootTo(origin)`** walks the internal parent links that were set when the child was inserted (`doc.data.section` → `section` map → `child`). Each link knows *how* it was reached (the property name, map key, or list index). This information is stored in hidden fields on the Item (`_parent`, `_key`, `_type`, `_index`, …) and is never exposed as a string.\n\n* The selector is therefore a **structured representation** of the location, not a dot‑separated string. When the Store later re‑plays the file (e.g. on reload), it can reconstruct the exact nesting by iterating the selector steps and creating/looking up the appropriate containers.\n\n* The final line that gets appended to the `.jsonl` file looks like (pretty‑printed for clarity):\n\n```json\n{\n  \"sel\": [\n    {\"type\":\"prop\",\"key\":\"data\"},\n    {\"type\":\"map\",\"key\":\"section\"}\n  ],\n  \"op\": {\"set\":{\"title\":\"Hello\"}}\n}\n```\n\nWhen the file is read back, the Store parses each line, walks the selector to locate the target Item, and applies the operation (`set` in this case). No `\"at\"` key or string path is ever needed; the selector array fully encodes the nesting relationship.",
